Yes. If Prometheus is running inside a Docker container, first enter the container and locate prometheus.yml.

Step 1: Find Prometheus Container
Shell
docker ps

Example output:

Plain Text
CONTAINER ID IMAGE NAMES
abcd1234 prom/prometheus prometheus
Step 2: Enter the Container
Shell
docker exec -it prometheus sh

or

Shell
docker exec -it prometheus /bin/sh
Step 3: Locate prometheus.yml

Usually it is here:

Shell
cd /etc/prometheus
ls

You should see:

Plain Text
prometheus.yml

View it:

Shell
cat /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Step 4: Add Node Exporter

Edit the file:

Shell
vi /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml

or

Shell
cat > /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
 

Add:

YAML
global:
scrape_interval: 15s
 
scrape_configs:
- job_name: "prometheus"
static_configs:
- targets: ["localhost:9090"]
 
- job_name: "node-exporter"
static_configs:
- targets: ["host.docker.internal:9100"]

If host.docker.internal doesn't work on Linux, use your VM IP:

YAML
- job_name: "node-exporter"
static_configs:
- targets: ["192.168.11.150:9100"]
Step 5: Restart Prometheus

Exit container:

Shell
exit

Restart:

Shell
docker restart prometheus
 
Step 6: Verify Target

Open:

Plain Text
http://localhost:9090/targets

You should now see:

Plain Text
prometheus UP
node-exporter UP
Step 7: Test in Grafana

Query:

PromQL
up

Expected:

Plain Text
up{job="prometheus"} 1
up{job="node-exporter"} 1

Then CPU panel:

PromQL
100 - (avg by(instance) (rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="idle"}[5m])) * 100)

Memory panel:

PromQL
((node_memory_MemTotal_bytes - node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes) / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes) * 100

If you're not sure where prometheus.yml is, run:

Shell
docker exec -it prometheus find / -name prometheus.yml 2>/dev/null

